<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>Read the denial letter closely. The stated reason decides which next step makes sense.</li>
<li>Ask in writing for the documents behind the decision: photos, estimates, engineer or inspection reports and the policy provisions cited.</li>
<li>Many denials involve wear and tear, pre-existing damage, late notice, exclusions or "not enough damage" findings, and some can be revisited with better evidence.</li>
<li>Appraisal resolves the <em>amount</em> of loss, not coverage. Coverage disputes and lawsuits belong with a Texas attorney.</li>
<li>Watch your deadlines. Policies often set time limits, and Texas law has pre-suit notice rules for weather claims.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Getting a <strong>denied insurance claim in San Marcos</strong> is frustrating, especially when you can see the damage from your driveway. But a denial letter is the insurer’s position at one point in time, based on the information it had. Sometimes that information was incomplete. This guide walks through why property claims are commonly denied, how to read the letter, what documents to request, and the realistic options for moving forward, from re-inspection to appraisal to a complaint with the Texas Department of Insurance.</p>

<p>It applies to homeowners and business owners in San Marcos, Hays County and nearby Central Texas communities. It is general information, not legal advice.</p>

<h2>Common reasons property claims are denied</h2>

<p>Denial letters tend to rely on a short list of reasons. Knowing which one you are dealing with helps you decide what evidence would actually matter.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Denial reason</th><th>What the insurer is saying</th><th>Evidence that may be relevant</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wear and tear / deterioration</td><td>The damage came from age, weathering or lack of maintenance, not a sudden event</td><td>Photos showing fresh fractures or impact marks, roof age records, maintenance history, storm data for your area</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pre-existing damage</td><td>The damage existed before the date of loss or before the policy began</td><td>Earlier photos, inspection reports from a home purchase, prior claim records, dated storm reports</td></tr>
<tr><td>Late notice</td><td>The claim was reported too long after the loss, which hurt the insurer’s ability to investigate</td><td>When you discovered the damage, when the storm occurred, any earlier contacts with the insurer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Policy exclusion</td><td>The cause of loss is excluded (for example flood, surface water, long-term leakage, mold, cosmetic damage)</td><td>The actual cause of the damage, the exact exclusion wording, any endorsements that change it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Not enough damage / below deductible</td><td>The covered damage is too minor, or the repair cost does not exceed the deductible</td><td>A detailed, line-item estimate; documentation of damage the inspection may have missed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Wear and tear vs. storm damage</h3>
<p>This is the most common argument on roof claims. Shingles age, lose granules and crack over time, and policies generally do not cover that. Hail and wind, however, leave patterns: random impact marks, bruising with exposed mat, creased or missing tabs, dented soft metals such as vents and gutters. Storm records help too. NOAA’s <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Storm Events Database</a> lists 180 hail reports in Hays County since 1967, with most in April and May. On <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181388" target="_blank" rel="noopener">May 9, 2024</a>, for example, NOAA recorded 3-inch hail at San Marcos Academy west of the city. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> and <a href="/storm-history/">storm history page</a> can help you find reports near your address.</p>

<h3>Exclusions that come up often in San Marcos</h3>
<p>Flood is the big one. Standard homeowners and commercial policies generally exclude flood, which comes instead from the National Flood Insurance Program or a private flood policy. In an area known for flash flooding, the line between covered water damage and excluded surface water matters a great deal. Long-term leaks, mold limits and cosmetic damage exclusions on metal roofs are other frequent issues. See our <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> pages for more.</p>

<h2>How to read your denial letter</h2>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/homeowner-reviewing-denial-letter.webp" alt="Worried homeowner reading a letter at a desk with a laptop and calculator" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Read the denial letter slowly and note every policy provision it cites.</figcaption></figure>

<p>Under the Texas <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prompt Payment of Claims statute</a>, an insurer that rejects a claim must state the reasons in its written notice. When you read the letter, look for:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>The stated reason.</strong> Is it a coverage reason (an exclusion) or a factual one (no storm damage found)?</li>
<li><strong>The policy language quoted.</strong> Write down every section, exclusion and endorsement cited. Then read them in your own policy, including the definitions.</li>
<li><strong>Who inspected and when.</strong> Was it a field adjuster, a ladder-assist inspector, an engineer, or a desk review of photos?</li>
<li><strong>Whether it is a full or partial denial.</strong> "We are paying for the gutters but not the roof" is a partial denial, and the options differ.</li>
<li><strong>Any deadlines or requests.</strong> Some letters invite you to submit additional information. Note any date mentioned.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you do not have a complete copy of your policy, ask the insurer or your agent for one, including the declarations page and every endorsement.</p>

<h2>Request the claim file and engineer report</h2>

<p>You cannot respond to evidence you have not seen. Send a written request to the adjuster asking for copies of:</p>

<ul>
<li>All photos taken during the inspection</li>
<li>The adjuster’s estimate and any roof or property diagrams</li>
<li>Any engineer, inspector or consultant report the decision relied on</li>
<li>The specific policy provisions and facts supporting the denial</li>
<li>Any weather or hail data the insurer used</li>
</ul>

<p>Insurers do not always share every internal document, but many will provide photos, estimates and reports when asked. Keep a copy of every request and response. If an engineer report concluded "no hail damage" or "thermal cracking," compare its photos and test squares with what you see on the roof. Reports sometimes inspect only one slope, overlook soft-metal damage or rely on photos taken in poor light.</p>

