<h2>Your options after a denial: a step-by-step table</h2>

<p>Not every option fits every denial. This table summarizes the common paths and when each one tends to fit.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What it involves</th><th>When it tends to fit</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1. Read the letter and policy</td><td>Identify the stated reason and the exact policy language</td><td>Every denial</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Request the claim file</td><td>Ask in writing for photos, estimates and reports</td><td>Every denial</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Get an independent inspection</td><td>A qualified contractor, public adjuster or engineer documents the damage</td><td>Factual disputes such as wear and tear, pre-existing or "no damage" findings</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Request reconsideration or re-inspection</td><td>Submit new evidence in writing and ask the insurer to inspect again</td><td>When you have evidence the insurer did not consider</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Invoke appraisal</td><td>Each side picks an appraiser, the appraisers pick an umpire, and an award agreed by two is binding on amount</td><td>Disputes over how much the covered loss is worth</td></tr>
<tr><td>6. File a TDI complaint</td><td>Ask the Texas Department of Insurance to review the insurer’s handling</td><td>Unanswered calls, missed deadlines, unexplained denials</td></tr>
<tr><td>7. Consult a Texas attorney</td><td>Legal advice on coverage, bad faith and lawsuits</td><td>Coverage disputes, approaching deadlines, or when other steps fail</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Re-inspection with better documentation</h3>
<p>A re-inspection is most useful when you bring something new: close-up photos of hail hits on shingles and soft metals, interior damage tied to a roof leak, a contractor’s written findings, dated storm reports, or photos from before the storm. Ask to be present, or have your representative present, and point out the damage directly. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists what to gather.</p>

<h3>Appraisal: for disputes over amount only</h3>
<p>Most Texas property policies include an appraisal clause. Either party can demand appraisal to resolve a disagreement over the amount of loss. It does not decide coverage. That means appraisal can help when the insurer agrees some damage is covered but values it far below your contractor’s estimate. It usually does not help when the insurer says nothing is covered. Read your policy’s appraisal provision for its exact terms, and see our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page.</p>

<h3>Complaint to the Texas Department of Insurance</h3>
<p>If an insurer stops responding, misses claim-handling deadlines, or will not explain its decision, you can file a complaint with the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/complfrm.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a>. TDI can ask the company to respond. It does not set the value of your claim or represent you, but a complaint can prompt a closer look at how the claim was handled.</p>

<h3>When to talk to an attorney</h3>
<p>Public adjusters are not attorneys and cannot give legal advice. If the denial rests on how an exclusion should be interpreted, if you believe the insurer acted in bad faith, or if a deadline is getting close, consult a Texas attorney. Weather-related property claims are subject to <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542A.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542A</a>, which requires written notice at least 61 days before filing suit. Many policies also contain a contractual deadline to sue, often two years and one day from when the claim accrued. An attorney can tell you what applies to your situation.</p>

<h2>Partial denials and underpayments</h2>

<p>Not every denial is a flat "no." Many San Marcos-area claims end with the insurer paying for some items, such as gutters, window screens or a few shingles, while denying the rest. Others are technically approved but paid at an amount that will not cover the repair. These situations call for a slightly different approach. Start by separating what was denied (a coverage or causation decision) from what was underpaid (a valuation decision). The denied portion is usually addressed with evidence about cause and coverage. The underpaid portion is usually addressed with a detailed estimate, measurements and photos, and, if the disagreement persists, possibly appraisal. Our <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> page explains how that review works.</p>

<h2>Mistakes to avoid after a denial</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Waiting.</strong> Deadlines in the policy keep running, and evidence fades as weather continues.</li>
<li><strong>Making permanent repairs before documenting.</strong> Do reasonable temporary repairs to prevent more damage, such as tarping, but photograph everything first and keep receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Relying on phone calls.</strong> Put requests and disagreements in writing so there is a record.</li>
<li><strong>Re-filing a new claim for the same loss</strong> without understanding how that interacts with the existing one. Ask first.</li>
<li><strong>Signing documents you do not understand,</strong> including releases, without reading them carefully.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How a San Marcos public adjuster can help with a denied claim</h2>

<p>When the dispute is about the damage itself, a <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> can inspect the property, compare the findings with the insurer’s report, prepare a detailed estimate, and present the evidence in writing. Public adjusters are licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance under Chapter 4102 of the Texas Insurance Code and represent the policyholder only. We cannot guarantee that a denial will be reversed, and some denials are correct under the policy. An honest review can tell you which situation you are in.</p>

<p>Our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page explains how we approach these files, and our guide on <a href="/why-property-insurance-claims-are-delayed-texas/">why property claims get delayed</a> covers claims that are stuck rather than denied. If you received a denial or a partial denial, a San Marcos public adjuster can take a look through our <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
